# Linear conventions

Linear is the work queue and audit record for this repository. It is not a
memory or knowledge store; recall belongs to `l9-graphiti-memory`. Do not
mirror repo state into Linear or treat issues as a cache.

## When to create an issue

Create a Linear issue when, and only when:

- a governance check fails and the fix is not immediate
- an invariant in `ORG_INVARIANTS.yaml` cannot be verified
- work is deferred, handed off, or spans sessions
- a decision needs a durable record

Do not create issues for transient shell errors, for work you complete in
the same turn, or as progress notes.

## Issue format

Title: `<area>: <imperative summary>` where area is a top-level directory
such as `autonomy`, `integrity`, `telemetry`, `execution-governance`.

Body must include:

- **Trigger** - what surfaced this (check name, invariant ID, file path)
- **Evidence** - the failing output or the specific lines, quoted
- **Scope** - files or directories expected to change
- **Blocked by** - other issue identifiers, if any

Always cite the invariant or policy ID when one applies. An issue without a
traceable trigger is not auditable.

## Reading before writing

Before creating an issue, search Linear for an existing one covering the same
trigger. Duplicates degrade the audit record. Prefer commenting on the
existing issue.

## Handoff protocol

When ending work that will resume in another session or on another machine,
write the resume state into the issue as a comment: what is done, what is
next, and the exact command to continue. Do not rely on chat context
surviving.

## Prohibitions

- Never close an issue you did not verify as resolved.
- Never paste secrets, tokens, connection strings, or customer data into an
  issue. Issues are readable by every connected MCP client.
- Never bulk-create issues. If more than three would be needed, stop and ask.
